\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces  Three-panel ecliptic-strip diagnostic for Main Belt Asteroids (MBA, orange) and Near-Earth Objects (NEO, blue) at the Spring Equinox epoch (2025 March 21) with $|\beta | < 3^\circ $, computed using K$|$M cloned orbits from S3M. \textit  {Top:} Ecliptic latitude $\beta $ versus longitude $\lambda $. \textit  {Middle:} Longitudinal rate of motion $v_\lambda $ (deg/day) versus $\lambda $. \textit  {Bottom:} Latitudinal rate of motion $v_\beta $ (deg/day) versus $\lambda $. The characteristic sinusoidal variation in $v_\lambda $ arises from the anti-solar observing geometry. MBAs occupy a dense, narrow band near $v_\lambda \approx -0.3$ to $0.0$~deg/day, while NEOs exhibit broader distributions extending to higher velocities, enabling population separation in $v_\lambda $--$v_\beta $ space. }}{4}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ecliptic_strip_mba_neo}{{2}{4}{Three-panel ecliptic-strip diagnostic for Main Belt Asteroids (MBA, orange) and Near-Earth Objects (NEO, blue) at the Spring Equinox epoch (2025 March 21) with $|\beta | < 3^\circ $, computed using K$|$M cloned orbits from S3M. \textit {Top:} Ecliptic latitude $\beta $ versus longitude $\lambda $. \textit {Middle:} Longitudinal rate of motion $v_\lambda $ (deg/day) versus $\lambda $. \textit {Bottom:} Latitudinal rate of motion $v_\beta $ (deg/day) versus $\lambda $. The characteristic sinusoidal variation in $v_\lambda $ arises from the anti-solar observing geometry. MBAs occupy a dense, narrow band near $v_\lambda \approx -0.3$ to $0.0$~deg/day, while NEOs exhibit broader distributions extending to higher velocities, enabling population separation in $v_\lambda $--$v_\beta $ space}{figure.caption.2}{}}
